preConditionHook:
// A SMALL BAR STOPPED AT A RECENT EXTREME: the bar's range is at most
// smallFraction of the ATR, its LOW sits within nearTicks of the lowest low of
// the extremeLookback bars ENDING ONE BAR AGO, and it closes up. Brooks reads
// the small bar at the level as the sellers having quit there. Short is the
// mirror at the highest high with a down close.
//
// Not CloseNearPriorLow, which measures the CLOSE against the SINGLE previous
// bar's low plus an offset: this measures the LOW against a WINDOW's lowest
// low, so the two read different prices off different bars. Two of the three
// terms are carried elsewhere (BarRangeAboveAtrMultiple negated, TrendBarBody
// at bodyFraction 0), but the proximity-to-a-recent-extreme term is not, and it
// is what makes the setup; Brooks's own test is a visual comparison with "a big
// bar", and the window low is the mechanizable reading.
//
// THE WINDOW ENDS ONE BAR AGO -- Lowest(Low, extremeLookback)[1] -- so the
// bar's own low cannot define the extreme it is being measured against. The
// scan mirrors EasyLanguage's Extremes(), which Highest() and Lowest() call:
// seeded at the window's first bar, scanning the rest, strict comparison, and
// EL SOURCE, so it carries the relational tolerance and uses el_lt / el_gt
// rather than raw < and >.
//
// ATR is MultiWalk's WFSafe_AvgTrueRange, a ROLLING accumulator seeded with a
// full sum on the first calculated bar and on a length change, then advanced as
// TWO statements -- EL evaluates S[1] + in - out left to right and folding it to
// S += in - out rounds differently (@OJ 2015-02-20). AtrProfitTarget is the
// reference implementation with the measurements. One call site, here.
// THE TICK is EL's own MinMove / PriceScale, both VERIFIED and per-symbol
// constants for the whole run, written DIVISION-FIRST in both twins so the C++
// reads token for token against its EasyLanguage (README "N ticks",
// TICKSIZE_FINDINGS.md). Read once into a local; it needs no history.
//
// Scratch below is declared in localVariables, not at hook scope: the generator
// only prefixes declared locals, so a hook-scope declaration is a redefinition
// when this rule is placed twice in one strategy. Every one is fully assigned
// each bar before it is read.
tickSz = ctx.MinMove() / ctx.PriceScale();
if (atrLength != (int)atrLookback) {
    atrSum = 0.0;
    for (int i = 0; i < (int)atrLookback; ++i) {
        double hi = high[i] > close[i + 1] ? high[i] : close[i + 1];
        double lo = low[i] < close[i + 1] ? low[i] : close[i + 1];
        atrSum += hi - lo;
    }
    atrLength = (int)atrLookback;
} else {
    const int out = (int)atrLookback;
    double hiIn = high[0] > close[1] ? high[0] : close[1];
    double loIn = low[0] < close[1] ? low[0] : close[1];
    double hiOut = high[out] > close[out + 1] ? high[out] : close[out + 1];
    double loOut = low[out] < close[out + 1] ? low[out] : close[out + 1];
    atrSum += hiIn - loIn;
    atrSum -= hiOut - loOut;
}
atrVal = atrSum / (int)atrLookback;
loWin = low[1];
hiWin = high[1];
for (int i = 2; i <= (int)extremeLookback; ++i) {
    if (el_lt(low[i], loWin)) loWin = low[i];
    if (el_gt(high[i], hiWin)) hiWin = high[i];
}
